<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<section xml:id="chp2sec1" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
    <title>Definition</title>
    <p>
        In your first course in linear algebra, you worked with <m>\R^n</m>, length <m>n</m> lists of real numbers.
        We called such lists vectors.
        We were able to <em>mix</em> vectors, or more precisely, add and scale vectors.
        These mixing properties allowed us to construct the concepts and tools of matrix algebra over <m>\R</m>: matrix equations, bases, decompositions, eigentheory, and more.
    </p>
    <p>
        We return to <m>\R^n</m> in the context of linear algebra, to perform structural abstraction and give life to an (abstract) vector space over a field.
    </p>
    <p>
        (I am trusting that you are comfortable with the concepts and tools from working with <m>\R^n</m> in your first course in linear algebra.
        This familiarity provides motivation, intuition, examples, and more generally, a robust concept image for what follows.
        I encourage you to consult my <em>Concepts of Linear Algebra (COLA)</em> text.
        Engage with the exposition, play with the GeoGebra manipulatives, and work out the homeworks.)
    </p>
    <definition xml:id="def-vectorspace">
        <title>Vector Space</title>
        <statement>
            <p>
                If <m>F</m> is a field, then a <term>vector space</term> over <m>F</m> is a set <m>V</m> with two maps which we call <term>addition</term> (<m>+</m>) and <term>scaling</term> <m>\cdot</m>:
            </p>
            <me>V\times V \to V;\ (\vect{v},\vect{w})\mapsto \vect{v}+\vect{w} \hspace{4pc} F\times V \to V;\ (a,\vect{v})\mapsto a\cdot \vect{v}</me>
            <p>
                satisfying:
            </p>
            <ol marker="(VS1)">
                <li>
                    <p>
                        Addition is <term>associative</term>:<br/>
                        If <m>\vect{u},\vect{v},\vect{w}\in V</m>, then <m>(\vect{u}+\vect{v})+\vect{w} = \vect{u}+(\vect{v}+\vect{w})</m>.
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        Addition has an <term>identity</term> (which we call a <term>zero vector</term>):<br/>
                        There exists an element <m>\vect{0}_V\in V</m> such that if <m>\vect{v}\in V</m>, then <m>\vect{0}_V+\vect{v} = \vect{v}</m>.
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        Addition has <term>inverses</term>:<br/>
                        If <m>\vect{v}\in V</m>, then there exists and element <m>\vect{w}\in V</m> such that <m>\vect{v}+\vect{w}=\vect{0}_V</m>.
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        Addition is <term>commutative</term>:<br/>
                        If <m>\vect{v},\vect{w}\in V</m>, then <m>\vect{v}+\vect{w} = \vect{w}+\vect{v}</m>.
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        Scaling is <term>associative</term>:<br/>
                        If <m>a,b\in F</m> and <m>\vect{v}\in V</m>, then <m>a\cdot (b \cdot \vect{v}) = (ab)\cdot \vect{v}</m>.
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        Scaling has an <term>identity</term>:<br/>
                        If <m>1\in F</m> is the multiplicative identity and <m>\vect{v}\in V</m>, then <m>1\cdot \vect{v} = \vect{v}</m>.
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        <term>Distributivity</term> of scaling 1:<br/>
                        If <m>a,b\in F</m> and <m>\vect{v}\in V</m>, then <m>(a+b)\cdot \vect{v} = a\cdot \vect{v} + b \cdot \vect{v}</m>.
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        <term>Distributivity</term> of scaling 2:<br/>
                        If <m>a\in F</m> and <m>\vect{v},\vect{w}\in V</m>, then <m>a \cdot (\vect{v}+\vect{w}) = a\cdot \vect{v} + a \cdot \vect{w}</m>.
                    </p>
                </li>
            </ol>
            <p>
                Elements of a vector space are called <term>vectors</term>. Elements of <m>F</m> are called <term>scalars</term>.
            </p>
        </statement>
    </definition>
    <p>
        To help distinguish vectors from scalars, when writing by hand, I will put an arrow (e.g. <m>\overrightarrow{v}</m>) over each vector, and when <m>\LaTeX</m> ing, I will always use <c>\vect{}</c> for each vector.
        When <m>V</m> is clear from context, I will write <m>\vect{0}</m> instead of <m>\vect{0}_V</m>.
    </p>


        <definition xml:id="def-linear_combination">
            <title>Linear Combination</title>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    If <m>S = \{\vect{v}_1,\ldots, \vect{v}_n\}\subset V</m>, then a <term>linear combination</term> of <m>S</m> is an expression of the form
                </p>
                <me>x_1\vect{v}_1+\cdots +x_n\vect{v}_n,</me>
                <p>
                    where <m>x_1,x_2,\ldots, x_n\in F</m>.
                </p>
                <p>
                    The <term>trivial</term> linear combination is the one with all 0 scalars. Otherwise it is <term>nontrivial</term>.
                </p>
            </statement>
        </definition>
        <p>
            Note that we only take <em>finite sums</em> in linear combinations.
        </p>


        <insight>
        <p>
            Addition is a <em>binary operation</em>, meaning it has two inputs. What does an expression like <m>\vect{u}+\vect{v}+\vect{w}</m> even mean?
        </p>      
        </insight>



</section>
